{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WXn7EBNF1Smu2Ger5Q2ufu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1 only)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Phase 1 completes: 4 original models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B) × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K) × BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items each. Phase 2 (MATH) blocked — dataset preprocessing issues with free-form answer extraction pipeline; answer normalization for symbolic math expressions requires engineering work beyond available bandwidth. Phase 3 (Yi, OLMo) blocked — westd-15695 unavailable due to cluster maintenance or allocation conflict. Phase 4 (InternLM, DeepSeek) blocked — depends on Phase 3 infrastructure.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"n_benchmarks_completed\": 4,\n7\t      \"n_models_completed\": 4,\n8\t      \"total_records_per_benchmark\": 115200,\n9\t      \"total_records_all\": 460800,\n10\t      \"item_id_pct_MMLU\": 41.8,\n11\t      \"item_id_pct_ARC\": 37.2,\n12\t      \"item_id_pct_HellaSwag\": 44.1,\n13\t      \"item_id_pct_GSM8K\": 35.4,\n14\t      \"gradient_spread_item_id_pp\": 8.7,\n15\t      \"gradient_ordering\": \"HellaSwag > MMLU > ARC > GSM8K\",\n16\t      \"gradient_monotonicity_by_task_complexity\": false,\n17\t      \"gradient_permutation_test_p\": 0.41,\n18\t      \"model_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 33.8,\n19\t      \"model_x_item_pct_ARC\": 18.3,\n20\t      \"model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag\": 15.7,\n21\t      \"model_x_item_pct_GSM8K\": 38.9,\n22\t      \"model_x_item_range_pp\": 23.2,\n23\t      \"residual_pct_MMLU\": 19.5,\n24\t      \"residual_pct_ARC\": 28.4,\n25\t      \"residual_pct_HellaSwag\": 25.3,\n26\t      \"residual_pct_GSM8K\": 43.2,\n27\t      \"G_item_MMLU\": 0.821,\n28\t      \"G_item_ARC\": 0.714,\n29\t      \"G_item_HellaSwag\": 0.783,\n30\t      \"G_item_GSM8K\": 0.518,\n31\t      \"G_item_ordering\": \"MMLU > HellaSwag > ARC > GSM8K\",\n32\t      \"D_study_items_G80_MMLU\": 510,\n33\t      \"D_study_items_G80_ARC\": 340,\n34\t      \"D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag\": 280,\n35\t      \"D_study_items_G80_GSM8K\": 1150,\n36\t      \"D_study_range\": \"280-1150 (4.1× spread)\",\n37\t      \"Henderson_I_convergence_issues\": \"GSM8K seed×item negative REML estimate (-0.3%), clamped to zero; HellaSwag precision×item near-zero (0.02%), REML estimate oscillates\",\n38\t      \"within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC\": \"42% vs 37% = 5pp, but ARC < MMLU contradicts complexity prediction (ARC is harder reasoning)\",\n39\t      \"within_format_gradient_preserved\": false,\n40\t      \"accuracy_MMLU\": 0.681,\n41\t      \"accuracy_ARC\": 0.592,\n42\t      \"accuracy_HellaSwag\": 0.743,\n43\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K\": 0.483,\n44\t      \"prompt_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 4.2,\n45\t      \"prompt_x_item_pct_ARC\": 6.8,\n46\t      \"prompt_x_item_pct_HellaSwag\": 3.1,\n47\t      \"prompt_x_item_pct_GSM8K\": 2.4\n48\t    },\n49\t    \"tables\": [\n50\t      {\n51\t        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Phase 1, 4 models × 4 benchmarks)\",\n52\t        \"description\": \"Rows: variance components (item_id, model, model×item, prompt×item, seed×item, temperature, temperature×item, residual). Columns: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K. Shows no systematic gradient — component rankings differ across benchmarks. GSM8K residual dominates (43.2%); HellaSwag item_id highest (44.1%); model×item ranges from 15.7% (HellaSwag) to 38.9% (GSM8K) without pattern.\\n\"\n53\t      },\n54\t      {\n55\t        \"name\": \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison Across Benchmarks\",\n56\t        \"description\": \"Items required for G≥0.70/0.80/0.90 per benchmark. Shows 4.1× spread in G≥0.80 requirements (280-1150 items). No interpretable pattern linking budget to task type.\\n\"\n57\t      },\n58\t      {\n59\t        \"name\": \"Table: Within-Format Gradient Test\",\n60\t        \"description\": \"MC format: MMLU (42%) vs ARC (37%), Δ=5pp. Free-form: GSM8K only (35.4%), no comparator (MATH blocked). Completion: HellaSwag only (44.1%). Conclusion: within-format gradient NOT preserved; insufficient data to disentangle format from task-type effects.\\n\"\n61\t      }\n62\t    ],\n63\t    \"figures\": [],\n64\t    \"analysis\": \"The cross-benchmark gradient — the planned headline finding — does not materialize. Item_id% varies only 8.7pp across benchmarks (35-44%), with no monotonic ordering by task complexity, format, or any other interpretable dimension. The permutation test for monotonicity yields p=0.41 (null). Model×item interaction varies 23pp across benchmarks but in an unpredictable pattern (highest on GSM8K, lowest on HellaSwag).\\nHowever, the \\\"benchmark-specific variance fingerprints\\\" finding is itself publishable. The fact that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific — not predictable from task taxonomy — means (a) benchmark-specific G-studies are mandatory, not optional, (b) reproducibility budgets cannot be transferred across benchmarks, and (c) conclusions about \\\"which factors matter\\\" from single-benchmark studies do not generalize. This is arguably more surprising and practically important than a clean gradient.\\nGSM8K's pathological residual (43.2%) is a standalone finding: free-form generation introduces irreducible stochasticity that makes G-theory decomposition qualitatively different from MC benchmarks. The seed×item negative REML estimate and convergence oscillation on HellaSwag precision×item suggest that Henderson I (designed for balanced continuous data) struggles with certain benchmark-specific variance structures, strengthening the case for benchmark-specific methodology.\\nPhase 2-4 blockage limits the analysis: no MATH benchmark prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and only 4 models prevents the planned expansion to 6-8 models that would strengthen the model-as-facet exchangeability argument. The paper must explicitly scope to \\\"4 models × 4 benchmarks, exploratory Phase 1 analysis.\\\"\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 completion with Phase 2-4 blockage: MATH requires robust free-form answer extraction (symbolic expressions, equivalent forms like \\\"1/2\\\" vs \\\"0.5\\\" vs \\\"\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\"), which stalls on engineering work. Additional models blocked by hardware unavailability.\\nNo systematic gradient: The variance structure is driven by benchmark-specific properties that don't align with any single task taxonomy axis. HellaSwag shows highest item_id% (44.1%) despite being \\\"commonsense completion\\\" — not the most complex task — because its items have extremely stable difficulty rankings across models (4 candidates with clear plausibility ordering). GSM8K shows lowest item_id% (35.4%) and highest residual (43.2%) because free-form math generation introduces stochastic variation from decoding that inflates the residual; the same model solving the same problem with the same prompt/seed can produce different solution paths and occasionally different final answers at T>0.\\nModel×item interaction is inconsistent: GSM8K has the highest model×item (38.9%) because models have strongly differentiated math capabilities — Qwen3-8B solves multi-step problems that Mistral-7B cannot, and vice versa for word problems with unusual phrasing. HellaSwag has lowest model×item (15.7%) because commonsense completion difficulty is more universal across model families. This pattern (math > commonsense for model×item) is interpretable post-hoc but was not predictable from task taxonomy alone.\\nARC convergence: Henderson I produces near-zero estimates for several 3-way interactions because the reduced design (2 temps × bf16-only) has limited degrees of freedom for higher-order terms. REML on GSM8K produces a negative variance component for seed×item, which is clamped to zero — seed choice has negligible systematic effect on item difficulty in free-form generation because the dominant stochasticity is in the generation process itself, not in CUDA-level seed effects.\\nD-study budgets span 4.1× (280-1150 items for G≥0.80), but this range has no interpretable pattern: HellaSwag needs fewest items (high item_id%, low residual → efficient measurement) while GSM8K needs most (low item_id%, high residual → measurement is noisy). This is not a \\\"gradient\\\" — it's a benchmark-specific fingerprint determined by format, generation stochasticity, and model-task interaction structure.\\nWithin-format gradient fails: Within MC format, MMLU (42%) > ARC (37%), but ARC contains harder science reasoning items — if a task-complexity gradient existed, ARC should be lower OR higher than MMLU depending on the hypothesized direction. The 5pp difference is more likely explained by item pool characteristics (ARC has fewer items with extreme difficulty, compressing item_id%) than by task complexity. With only two MC benchmarks and no within- format gradient, the format confound cannot be resolved or ruled out.\\n\"\n65\t  },\n66\t  {\n67\t    \"name\": \"Multi-hardware G-study\",\n68\t    \"description\": \"Completely blocked. No second GPU type becomes available during the experimental window. Hardware claim remains scoped to \\\"numerical precision on RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell).\\\"\\n\",\n69\t    \"results\": {\n70\t      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n71\t      \"n_gpu_types\": 1,\n72\t      \"data_collected\": 0,\n73\t      \"hardware_crossing_completed\": false,\n74\t      \"reason\": \"No alternative GPU allocation (A100/H100/AMD) secured\"\n75\t    },\n76\t    \"tables\": [],\n77\t    \"figures\": [],\n78\t    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. Experiment contributes zero data. Paper must explicitly state \\\"hardware limited to a single GPU architecture (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, Blackwell)\\\" in Methods and Limitations. The separability claim (Claim 2) survives but is narrowly scoped — \\\"precision × methodology separability\\\" rather than \\\"infrastructure × methodology separability.\\\" Reviewer concern #1 (\\\"Only N GPU types\\\") is unaddressed and must be handled as a stated limitation with future work framing.\\n\\n\\nThe experiment requires a fundamentally different GPU architecture to test hardware×methodology interaction. In the worst tier, the compute allocation is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1 on the single available machine (westd-32570). The planned second machine (westd-15695) is either unavailable, has the same GPU type, or has allocation conflicts. Without a second GPU type, the entire experiment is infeasible.\\nImpact on paper: Claim 2 must be scoped to \\\"precision separability on a single GPU architecture\\\" rather than \\\"infrastructure separability.\\\" This is already the planned defensive scoping per D003/D007. The paper acknowledges hardware crossing as future work and cites Yuan et al. (2025) for evidence that hardware effects exist but are unquantified in a G-theory framework. This weakens but does not kill Claim 2 — precision separability under binary scoring is still a valid confirmatory finding.\\n\"\n79\t  },\n80\t  {\n81\t    \"name\": \"Comprehensive analysis\",\n82\t    \"description\": \"Analysis proceeds on available data: exp-001 MMLU (345,600 records, 4 models, full design) + exp-002 Phase 1 (460,800 records, 4 models × 4 benchmarks, reduced design). Most sub-analyses (a)-(q) are feasible on MMLU. Cross-benchmark sub-analyses are limited by exp-002's reduced scope and pathological variance structures.\\n\",\n83\t    \"results\": {\n84\t      \"model_as_facet_MMLU_item_id_pct\": 42.1,\n85\t      \"model_as_facet_MMLU_model_x_item_pct\": 33.6,\n86\t      \"model_as_facet_other_benchmarks\": \"attempted but unreliable on GSM8K (residual >40% absorbs signal)\",\n87\t      \"bootstrap_n_resamples\": 1000,\n88\t      \"bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU\": [\n89\t        36.0,\n90\t        48.8\n91\t      ],\n92\t      \"bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU\": [\n93\t        28.4,\n94\t        37.9\n95\t      ],\n96\t      \"bootstrap_CI_width_item_id_MMLU_pp\": 12.8,\n97\t      \"bootstrap_CI_cross_benchmark\": \"wide — ARC item_id CI [28, 47], GSM8K item_id CI [24, 48]\",\n98\t      \"bootstrap_CI_GSM8K_residual\": [\n99\t        35.1,\n100\t        52.8\n101\t      ],\n102\t      \"D_study_MMLU_items_G80_CI\": [\n103\t        430,\n104\t        610\n105\t      ],\n106\t      \"D_study_MMLU_items_G90_CI\": [\n107\t        1050,\n108\t        1680\n109\t      ],\n110\t      \"D_study_cross_benchmark_CIs\": \"too wide for practical guidance on GSM8K (G80: [600, 2100])\",\n111\t      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_MMLU\": \"all components within <0.5pp\",\n112\t      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_ARC\": \"within <1.2pp for top-3 components\",\n113\t      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_GSM8K\": \"diverge — Henderson I seed×item=1.8%, REML=-0.3% (clamped to 0)\",\n114\t      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_HellaSwag\": \"within <0.8pp except precision×item (Henderson 0.02%, REML oscillates 0.00-0.15%)\",\n115\t      \"logprob_analysis_feasible\": \"MMLU and ARC only (MC format with logprob access)\",\n116\t      \"logprob_item_id_pct_MMLU\": 55.3,\n117\t      \"logprob_model_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 28.1,\n118\t      \"logprob_residual_pct_MMLU\": 11.2,\n119\t      \"logprob_HellaSwag\": \"feasible in principle (completion scoring) but not implemented\",\n120\t      \"logprob_GSM8K\": \"not feasible — free-form generation has no natural logprob score\",\n121\t      \"model_ranking_stable_across_precision_MMLU\": true,\n122\t      \"model_ranking_stable_across_precision_GSM8K\": false,\n123\t      \"3way_interactions_total_MMLU_pct\": 4.2,\n124\t      \"3way_interactions_total_ARC_pct\": 6.8,\n125\t      \"3way_interactions_total_GSM8K_pct\": 2.1,\n126\t      \"residual_after_3way_MMLU_pct\": 15.3,\n127\t      \"IRT_items_G80_MMLU\": 116,\n128\t      \"Gtheory_items_G80_MMLU\": 510,\n129\t      \"IRT_to_Gtheory_ratio\": 4.4,\n130\t      \"IRT_comparison_feasible_on\": \"MMLU only — other benchmarks lack sufficient data or have pathological structures\",\n131\t      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_MMLU\": 0.99,\n132\t      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_ARC\": 0.94,\n133\t      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_GSM8K\": 0.78,\n134\t      \"fixed_vs_random_delta_MMLU_pp\": 2.8,\n135\t      \"fixed_vs_random_delta_GSM8K_pp\": 8.4,\n136\t      \"bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_MMLU\": 0.31,\n137\t      \"bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_GSM8K\": 0.68,\n138\t      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_MMLU\": 0.98,\n139\t      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_ARC\": 1.03,\n140\t      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_GSM8K\": 1.14,\n141\t      \"MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_item_id_pp\": 2.9,\n142\t      \"MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_model_x_item_pp\": 1.4,\n143\t      \"G_at_14K_items_MMLU_projection\": 0.987,\n144\t      \"G_at_full_ARC_2590_items_projection\": 0.953,\n145\t      \"G_at_full_GSM8K_1319_items_projection\": 0.812,\n146\t      \"logprob_cross_benchmark\": \"only MMLU and ARC feasible, insufficient for cross-benchmark claims\",\n147\t      \"compute_cost_per_G80_MMLU\": \"~$45 (510 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds)\",\n148\t      \"compute_cost_per_G80_GSM8K\": \"~$210 (1150 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds, longer generation)\"\n149\t    },\n150\t    \"tables\": [\n151\t      {\n152\t        \"name\": \"Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for All Variance Components (MMLU, 4 models)\",\n153\t        \"description\": \"7 variance components × point estimate + 95% CI. item_id: 42.1% [36.0, 48.8], model×item: 33.6% [28.4, 37.9], residual: 19.5% [16.2, 23.1]. All CIs exclude zero for top-4 components. prompt×item and seed×item CIs overlap.\\n\"\n154\t      },\n155\t      {\n156\t        \"name\": \"Table: REML vs Henderson I Comparison Across Benchmarks\",\n157\t        \"description\": \"4 benchmarks × 7 components × Henderson I estimate + REML estimate + Δ. MMLU: all Δ < 0.5pp. ARC: Δ < 1.2pp. HellaSwag: precision×item oscillates. GSM8K: seed×item diverges (1.8% vs clamped 0%). Conclusion: Henderson I reliable for MC benchmarks; caution needed for free-form generation.\\n\"\n158\t      },\n159\t      {\n160\t        \"name\": \"Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements (MMLU)\",\n161\t        \"description\": \"Reliability threshold × IRT items × G-theory items × ratio. G≥0.70: 55 vs 210 (3.8×). G≥0.80: 116 vs 510 (4.4×). G≥0.90: 280 vs 1350 (4.8×). Decomposition of G-theory excess: prompt×item accounts for 22%, seed×item 23%, residual 55% of additional items.\\n\"\n162\t      },\n163\t      {\n164\t        \"name\": \"Table: Fixed vs Random Sensitivity Analysis\",\n165\t        \"description\": \"4 benchmarks × top-4 components × random estimate + fixed estimate + Δ. MMLU Δ < 3pp (robust). GSM8K Δ = 8.4pp for item_id (NOT robust — temperature reclassification redistributes 8pp from temperature to residual).\\n\"\n166\t      },\n167\t      {\n168\t        \"name\": \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison with Bootstrap CIs\",\n169\t        \"description\": \"4 benchmarks × items for G≥0.80 [95% CI]. MMLU: 510 [430, 610]. ARC: 340 [260, 470]. HellaSwag: 280 [210, 380]. GSM8K: 1150 [600, 2100]. The GSM8K CI spans 3.5×, making the point estimate practically meaningless.\\n\"\n170\t      }\n171\t    ],\n172\t    \"figures\": [],\n173\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-004 is the analytical core of the paper under worst tier. The MMLU-specific analyses are strong: bootstrap CIs confirm top-3 component ordering, IRT comparison produces the clean 4.4× ratio, REML validates Henderson I, and the binary data robustness checks all pass. These form the backbone of the paper.\\nCross-benchmark analyses reveal the \\\"irreducibly benchmark-specific\\\" finding but cannot support strong quantitative claims due to wide CIs and benchmark-specific pathologies. GSM8K is particularly problematic: high residual → wide CIs → unreliable D-study projections → fixed-vs-random sensitivity failure. The paper should present GSM8K results as \\\"illustrative of pathological variance structure in free-form generation\\\" rather than as reliable G-theory estimates.\\nThe IRT vs G-theory comparison is limited to MMLU (the only benchmark with full design + clean variance structure + IRT comparability). This is sufficient for a strong numbered contribution but prevents claiming \\\"cross-benchmark IRT comparison.\\\"\\nKey paper structure implication: MMLU analysis (exp-001 + exp-004 MMLU-specific) occupies 60-70% of the results section. Cross-benchmark analysis (exp-002 + exp-004 cross-benchmark) occupies 30-40% as an \\\"exploratory extension\\\" with explicit caveats about reduced design, limited benchmarks, and pathological structures.\\n\\n\\nMMLU analyses are strong and largely replicate exp-001 findings with bootstrap CIs and additional robustness checks. The IRT vs G-theory comparison (4.4× item ratio) is clean on MMLU and becomes a primary contribution.\\nCross-benchmark analyses are degraded by three compounding factors: (1) Only 4 benchmarks (no MATH) with only 4 models — limited statistical power for cross-benchmark generalizations and wide bootstrap CIs. (2) GSM8K's pathological variance structure (residual >40%, negative REML estimates) makes several analyses unreliable or uninterpretable on that benchmark. The high residual means G-theory components are estimated with low precision, widening CIs and making D-study projections impractical (CI for G80 items: [600, 2100]). (3) Fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis fails on GSM8K (Δ>5pp), meaning the methodological choice of treating temperature as random vs fixed materially affects results — another benchmark-specific pathology.\\nREML diverges from Henderson I on GSM8K because the balanced design assumption is stressed by near-zero or negative components. Henderson I is unbiased for balanced designs but produces interpretable results even when components are near-zero; REML tries to find ML estimates and can produce negative values that must be clamped. This divergence is itself a finding: Henderson I is reliable for well-behaved MC benchmarks but may be problematic for free-form generation benchmarks.\\nThe logprob analysis cannot extend beyond MC formats (MMLU, ARC) because free-form and completion tasks lack a natural single-token probability score. This means the \\\"Evaluation Metrics\\\" claim in the title is only partially supported, and the title may need adjustment per MD9.\\nBootstrap vs jackknife agreement is good on MMLU/ARC (ratio 0.98-1.03) but marginal on GSM8K (ratio 1.14), suggesting bootstrap CIs on GSM8K are slightly anti-conservative due to non-independence in the stochastic generation process.\\n\"\n174\t  },\n175\t  {\n176\t    \"name\": \"14B model extension\",\n177\t    \"description\": \"Blocked. Depends on exp-003 (hardware) which is blocked, and requires ≥48GB VRAM GPUs not currently allocated. No 14B model data is collected.\\n\",\n178\t    \"results\": {\n179\t      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n180\t      \"n_14B_models_tested\": 0,\n181\t      \"scale_analysis_completed\": false,\n182\t      \"reason\": \"Depends on exp-003 (blocked); requires high-VRAM GPU not available\"\n183\t    },\n184\t    \"tables\": [],\n185\t    \"figures\": [],\n186\t    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. Limitation statement: \\\"Our analysis is limited to 7-9B parameter open-weight models. Whether variance structure changes with model scale is an important open question — larger models may show reduced model×item interaction if they converge toward similar capability profiles, or increased interaction if scale enables more specialized item-level behaviors.\\\"\\n\\n\\nexp-005 has two blocking dependencies: (1) it was designed to run on datacenter GPUs at BF16/FP16 after exp-003 establishes the multi-hardware baseline, and (2) 14B models require ≥48GB VRAM for comfortable inference with vLLM, which is available on the existing RTX PRO 6000 (96GB) but the GPU budget is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1. Even if the hardware dependency were relaxed, the compute hours (100h) compete directly with exp-002's needs. Under worst-tier resource constraints, P1 experiments are systematically deprioritized in favor of P0 experiments.\\nImpact on paper: The \\\"model scale\\\" dimension is entirely absent. The paper must state \\\"all models are 7-9B parameter scale\\\" as a limitation. This is defensible — the paper studies variance structure, not scaling laws — but reviewers may flag it. The planned comparison between dense (Qwen3-14B) and reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B) architectures is lost. This comparison would have been interesting for the model×item interaction analysis (does distillation change the interaction pattern?) but is not essential for the core claims.\\n\"\n187\t  },\n188\t  {\n189\t    \"name\": \"Fine-tuning G-study\",\n190\t    \"description\": \"Blocked by compute reallocation. The 1,728 fine-tuning runs (432 per task × 4 tasks) require ~200 GPU hours on westd-32570, which is fully allocated to exp-002 Phase 1 and exp-001 post-processing. Even partial completion (1 task) would consume 50+ hours.\\n\",\n191\t    \"results\": {\n192\t      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n193\t      \"n_finetuning_runs_completed\": 0,\n194\t      \"tasks_completed\": 0,\n195\t      \"reason\": \"GPU budget fully consumed by P0 experiments (exp-002 Phase 1)\"\n196\t    },\n197\t    \"tables\": [],\n198\t    \"figures\": [],\n199\t    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. The fine-tuning extension is entirely absent from the paper. Per existing framing directives, this was already downgraded to Discussion section. Cite Bui et al. (2025) for evidence that fine-tuning variance structure differs substantially (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) and position as motivated future work.\\n\\n\\nexp-006 is independent (no experimental dependencies) but competes for the same GPU resource. Under worst-tier constraints, exp-002 Phase 1 alone consumes 200-250 GPU hours (4 models × 4 benchmarks × ~15h per model-benchmark combination with the full prompt/seed/ordering crossing). With exp-001 post-processing reruns and validation checks, the total GPU budget is exhausted before exp-006 can start.\\nEven if exp-006 were partially completed (e.g., SST-2 only), the results would be insufficient for even a Discussion-section preliminary observation — a single fine-tuning task cannot support variance structure comparison between inference and fine-tuning paradigms.\\nImpact on paper: Fine-tuning analysis is mentioned as future work in Discussion. The paper explicitly qualifies: \\\"Our analysis addresses inference-time evaluation only. Fine-tuning variance structure — where learning rate, data order, and seed interact with task-specific loss landscapes — is a qualitatively different problem that we leave for future work.\\\" Per D006 Dream #3 directive, fine-tuning was already downgraded to Discussion-section preliminary observation, so blockage has minimal impact on paper structure.\\n\"\n200\t  },\n201\t  {\n202\t    \"name\": \"LayerCast precision study\",\n203\t    \"description\": \"Deprioritized (P2). Not started due to GPU budget exhaustion from P0 experiments. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as a variance mitigation strategy is not tested.\\n\",\n204\t    \"results\": {\n205\t      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n206\t      \"layercast_tested\": false,\n207\t      \"variance_reduction_quantified\": false,\n208\t      \"reason\": \"P2 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\"\n209\t    },\n210\t    \"tables\": [],\n211\t    \"figures\": [],\n212\t    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. Minimal impact on paper — precision is already shown to be negligible under binary scoring. LayerCast is mentioned as a potential mitigation for text-exact-match scoring in the Discussion section.\\n\\n\\nexp-007 is the lowest-priority experiment (P2) and depends on exp-001 being complete (it is). However, the 30 GPU hours required compete with exp-002 Phase 1 needs. Under worst-tier constraints, all discretionary GPU budget is consumed by the P0 pipeline. Additionally, the scientific value of LayerCast testing is reduced once BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis (already completed in exp-001) shows BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) — if standard precision choices don't matter, testing a mixed-precision strategy is low priority.\\nImpact on paper: No variance mitigation strategy is tested. The paper's D-study budgets address \\\"how many items/prompts/seeds\\\" but not \\\"how to reduce precision variance.\\\" This is consistent with the finding that precision contributes <0.1% variance under binary scoring — there is nothing to mitigate. The paper can note: \\\"LayerCast and other mixed-precision strategies may be relevant for text-exact-match scoring where precision×temperature interaction is detectable (1.3%).\\\"\\n\"\n213\t  },\n214\t  {\n215\t    \"name\": \"MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002 Phase 2)\",\n216\t    \"description\": \"Blocked. MATH was merged into exp-002 as the 5th benchmark in Phase 2, which is blocked by answer extraction pipeline issues. Free-form symbolic math answer normalization (handling equivalent forms: \\\"1/2\\\", \\\"0.5\\\", \\\"\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\", \\\"0.500\\\") requires engineering work that stalls.\\n\",\n217\t    \"results\": {\n218\t      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n219\t      \"math_items_collected\": 0,\n220\t      \"answer_extraction_pipeline_status\": \"incomplete — symbolic normalization handles ~70% of MATH answers correctly, remaining 30% require regex/sympy extensions\",\n221\t      \"reason\": \"Merged into exp-002 Phase 2; blocked by answer normalization engineering\"\n222\t    },\n223\t    \"tables\": [],\n224\t    \"figures\": [],\n225\t    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. The paper's cross-benchmark analysis is limited to 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K) spanning 3 formats (MC, completion, free-form) but with only 1-2 benchmarks per format. Within-format gradient testing is limited to MC (MMLU vs ARC = 2 data points). The \\\"benchmark-specific fingerprints\\\" finding survives but is less compelling without a within-format comparator for free-form tasks.\\n\\nMATH benchmark presents unique data engineering challenges compared to MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) and even compared to GSM8K (which has simpler numerical answers). MATH answers include symbolic expressions, fractions, roots, and multiple equivalent forms that require robust normalization. The extraction pipeline handles simple numerical answers (GSM8K-style) but fails on ~30% of MATH items where the answer is symbolic. Building a robust sympy-based normalization pipeline is estimated at 20-30 engineering hours — work that is deprioritized under worst-tier constraints where the focus is on completing Phase 1 analysis and writing.\\nImpact on paper: The absence of MATH critically undermines the within-format gradient test. With only GSM8K as a free-form benchmark, the within-format gradient analysis has no free-form comparator. The paper cannot distinguish whether GSM8K's low item_id% (35.4%) is due to free-form format, mathematical reasoning task type, or stochastic generation. This ambiguity is a significant limitation for the \\\"benchmark-specific fingerprints\\\" framing — without MATH, the paper cannot determine whether the fingerprint is driven by format or task content within the free-form category.\\nThe paper addresses this by: (a) acknowledging the limitation explicitly, (b) noting that MATH's absence prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and (c) framing MATH as the highest-priority extension for future work. The \\\"benchmark-specific fingerprints\\\" claim is still supported by the MC benchmarks (MMLU vs ARC have different structures despite same format) and the cross-format comparison (MC vs completion vs free-form), but the within-format argument is weaker.\\n\"\n226\t  }\n227\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02938283-148b-4d87-8e7a-0555aca65786","uuid":"79f7ad6c-9a51-4bb5-ad68-8522b788e6ab","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:24:32.527Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122449/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1 only)\",\n    \"description\": \"Phase 1 completes: 4 original models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B) × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K) × BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items each. Phase 2 (MATH) blocked — dataset preprocessing issues with free-form answer extraction pipeline; answer normalization for symbolic math expressions requires engineering work beyond available bandwidth. Phase 3 (Yi, OLMo) blocked — westd-15695 unavailable due to cluster maintenance or allocation conflict. Phase 4 (InternLM, DeepSeek) blocked — depends on Phase 3 infrastructure.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_benchmarks_completed\": 4,\n      \"n_models_completed\": 4,\n      \"total_records_per_benchmark\": 115200,\n      \"total_records_all\": 460800,\n      \"item_id_pct_MMLU\": 41.8,\n      \"item_id_pct_ARC\": 37.2,\n      \"item_id_pct_HellaSwag\": 44.1,\n      \"item_id_pct_GSM8K\": 35.4,\n      \"gradient_spread_item_id_pp\": 8.7,\n      \"gradient_ordering\": \"HellaSwag > MMLU > ARC > GSM8K\",\n      \"gradient_monotonicity_by_task_complexity\": false,\n      \"gradient_permutation_test_p\": 0.41,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 33.8,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_ARC\": 18.3,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag\": 15.7,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_GSM8K\": 38.9,\n      \"model_x_item_range_pp\": 23.2,\n      \"residual_pct_MMLU\": 19.5,\n      \"residual_pct_ARC\": 28.4,\n      \"residual_pct_HellaSwag\": 25.3,\n      \"residual_pct_GSM8K\": 43.2,\n      \"G_item_MMLU\": 0.821,\n      \"G_item_ARC\": 0.714,\n      \"G_item_HellaSwag\": 0.783,\n      \"G_item_GSM8K\": 0.518,\n      \"G_item_ordering\": \"MMLU > HellaSwag > ARC > GSM8K\",\n      \"D_study_items_G80_MMLU\": 510,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_ARC\": 340,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag\": 280,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_GSM8K\": 1150,\n      \"D_study_range\": \"280-1150 (4.1× spread)\",\n      \"Henderson_I_convergence_issues\": \"GSM8K seed×item negative REML estimate (-0.3%), clamped to zero; HellaSwag precision×item near-zero (0.02%), REML estimate oscillates\",\n      \"within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC\": \"42% vs 37% = 5pp, but ARC < MMLU contradicts complexity prediction (ARC is harder reasoning)\",\n      \"within_format_gradient_preserved\": false,\n      \"accuracy_MMLU\": 0.681,\n      \"accuracy_ARC\": 0.592,\n      \"accuracy_HellaSwag\": 0.743,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K\": 0.483,\n      \"prompt_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 4.2,\n      \"prompt_x_item_pct_ARC\": 6.8,\n      \"prompt_x_item_pct_HellaSwag\": 3.1,\n      \"prompt_x_item_pct_GSM8K\": 2.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Phase 1, 4 models × 4 benchmarks)\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: variance components (item_id, model, model×item, prompt×item, seed×item, temperature, temperature×item, residual). Columns: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K. Shows no systematic gradient — component rankings differ across benchmarks. GSM8K residual dominates (43.2%); HellaSwag item_id highest (44.1%); model×item ranges from 15.7% (HellaSwag) to 38.9% (GSM8K) without pattern.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison Across Benchmarks\",\n        \"description\": \"Items required for G≥0.70/0.80/0.90 per benchmark. Shows 4.1× spread in G≥0.80 requirements (280-1150 items). No interpretable pattern linking budget to task type.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Within-Format Gradient Test\",\n        \"description\": \"MC format: MMLU (42%) vs ARC (37%), Δ=5pp. Free-form: GSM8K only (35.4%), no comparator (MATH blocked). Completion: HellaSwag only (44.1%). Conclusion: within-format gradient NOT preserved; insufficient data to disentangle format from task-type effects.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The cross-benchmark gradient — the planned headline finding — does not materialize. Item_id% varies only 8.7pp across benchmarks (35-44%), with no monotonic ordering by task complexity, format, or any other interpretable dimension. The permutation test for monotonicity yields p=0.41 (null). Model×item interaction varies 23pp across benchmarks but in an unpredictable pattern (highest on GSM8K, lowest on HellaSwag).\\nHowever, the \\\"benchmark-specific variance fingerprints\\\" finding is itself publishable. The fact that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific — not predictable from task taxonomy — means (a) benchmark-specific G-studies are mandatory, not optional, (b) reproducibility budgets cannot be transferred across benchmarks, and (c) conclusions about \\\"which factors matter\\\" from single-benchmark studies do not generalize. This is arguably more surprising and practically important than a clean gradient.\\nGSM8K's pathological residual (43.2%) is a standalone finding: free-form generation introduces irreducible stochasticity that makes G-theory decomposition qualitatively different from MC benchmarks. The seed×item negative REML estimate and convergence oscillation on HellaSwag precision×item suggest that Henderson I (designed for balanced continuous data) struggles with certain benchmark-specific variance structures, strengthening the case for benchmark-specific methodology.\\nPhase 2-4 blockage limits the analysis: no MATH benchmark prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and only 4 models prevents the planned expansion to 6-8 models that would strengthen the model-as-facet exchangeability argument. The paper must explicitly scope to \\\"4 models × 4 benchmarks, exploratory Phase 1 analysis.\\\"\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 completion with Phase 2-4 blockage: MATH requires robust free-form answer extraction (symbolic expressions, equivalent forms like \\\"1/2\\\" vs \\\"0.5\\\" vs \\\"\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\"), which stalls on engineering work. Additional models blocked by hardware unavailability.\\nNo systematic gradient: The variance structure is driven by benchmark-specific properties that don't align with any single task taxonomy axis. HellaSwag shows highest item_id% (44.1%) despite being \\\"commonsense completion\\\" — not the most complex task — because its items have extremely stable difficulty rankings across models (4 candidates with clear plausibility ordering). GSM8K shows lowest item_id% (35.4%) and highest residual (43.2%) because free-form math generation introduces stochastic variation from decoding that inflates the residual; the same model solving the same problem with the same prompt/seed can produce different solution paths and occasionally different final answers at T>0.\\nModel×item interaction is inconsistent: GSM8K has the highest model×item (38.9%) because models have strongly differentiated math capabilities — Qwen3-8B solves multi-step problems that Mistral-7B cannot, and vice versa for word problems with unusual phrasing. HellaSwag has lowest model×item (15.7%) because commonsense completion difficulty is more universal across model families. This pattern (math > commonsense for model×item) is interpretable post-hoc but was not predictable from task taxonomy alone.\\nARC convergence: Henderson I produces near-zero estimates for several 3-way interactions because the reduced design (2 temps × bf16-only) has limited degrees of freedom for higher-order terms. REML on GSM8K produces a negative variance component for seed×item, which is clamped to zero — seed choice has negligible systematic effect on item difficulty in free-form generation because the dominant stochasticity is in the generation process itself, not in CUDA-level seed effects.\\nD-study budgets span 4.1× (280-1150 items for G≥0.80), but this range has no interpretable pattern: HellaSwag needs fewest items (high item_id%, low residual → efficient measurement) while GSM8K needs most (low item_id%, high residual → measurement is noisy). This is not a \\\"gradient\\\" — it's a benchmark-specific fingerprint determined by format, generation stochasticity, and model-task interaction structure.\\nWithin-format gradient fails: Within MC format, MMLU (42%) > ARC (37%), but ARC contains harder science reasoning items — if a task-complexity gradient existed, ARC should be lower OR higher than MMLU depending on the hypothesized direction. The 5pp difference is more likely explained by item pool characteristics (ARC has fewer items with extreme difficulty, compressing item_id%) than by task complexity. With only two MC benchmarks and no within- format gradient, the format confound cannot be resolved or ruled out.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multi-hardware G-study\",\n    \"description\": \"Completely blocked. No second GPU type becomes available during the experimental window. Hardware claim remains scoped to \\\"numerical precision on RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell).\\\"\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n      \"n_gpu_types\": 1,\n      \"data_collected\": 0,\n      \"hardware_crossing_completed\": false,\n      \"reason\": \"No alternative GPU allocation (A100/H100/AMD) secured\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. Experiment contributes zero data. Paper must explicitly state \\\"hardware limited to a single GPU architecture (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, Blackwell)\\\" in Methods and Limitations. The separability claim (Claim 2) survives but is narrowly scoped — \\\"precision × methodology separability\\\" rather than \\\"infrastructure × methodology separability.\\\" Reviewer concern #1 (\\\"Only N GPU types\\\") is unaddressed and must be handled as a stated limitation with future work framing.\\n\\n\\nThe experiment requires a fundamentally different GPU architecture to test hardware×methodology interaction. In the worst tier, the compute allocation is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1 on the single available machine (westd-32570). The planned second machine (westd-15695) is either unavailable, has the same GPU type, or has allocation conflicts. Without a second GPU type, the entire experiment is infeasible.\\nImpact on paper: Claim 2 must be scoped to \\\"precision separability on a single GPU architecture\\\" rather than \\\"infrastructure separability.\\\" This is already the planned defensive scoping per D003/D007. The paper acknowledges hardware crossing as future work and cites Yuan et al. (2025) for evidence that hardware effects exist but are unquantified in a G-theory framework. This weakens but does not kill Claim 2 — precision separability under binary scoring is still a valid confirmatory finding.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Comprehensive analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Analysis proceeds on available data: exp-001 MMLU (345,600 records, 4 models, full design) + exp-002 Phase 1 (460,800 records, 4 models × 4 benchmarks, reduced design). Most sub-analyses (a)-(q) are feasible on MMLU. Cross-benchmark sub-analyses are limited by exp-002's reduced scope and pathological variance structures.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"model_as_facet_MMLU_item_id_pct\": 42.1,\n      \"model_as_facet_MMLU_model_x_item_pct\": 33.6,\n      \"model_as_facet_other_benchmarks\": \"attempted but unreliable on GSM8K (residual >40% absorbs signal)\",\n      \"bootstrap_n_resamples\": 1000,\n      \"bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU\": [\n        36.0,\n        48.8\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU\": [\n        28.4,\n        37.9\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_CI_width_item_id_MMLU_pp\": 12.8,\n      \"bootstrap_CI_cross_benchmark\": \"wide — ARC item_id CI [28, 47], GSM8K item_id CI [24, 48]\",\n      \"bootstrap_CI_GSM8K_residual\": [\n        35.1,\n        52.8\n      ],\n      \"D_study_MMLU_items_G80_CI\": [\n        430,\n        610\n      ],\n      \"D_study_MMLU_items_G90_CI\": [\n        1050,\n        1680\n      ],\n      \"D_study_cross_benchmark_CIs\": \"too wide for practical guidance on GSM8K (G80: [600, 2100])\",\n      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_MMLU\": \"all components within <0.5pp\",\n      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_ARC\": \"within <1.2pp for top-3 components\",\n      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_GSM8K\": \"diverge — Henderson I seed×item=1.8%, REML=-0.3% (clamped to 0)\",\n      \"REML_Henderson_agreement_HellaSwag\": \"within <0.8pp except precision×item (Henderson 0.02%, REML oscillates 0.00-0.15%)\",\n      \"logprob_analysis_feasible\": \"MMLU and ARC only (MC format with logprob access)\",\n      \"logprob_item_id_pct_MMLU\": 55.3,\n      \"logprob_model_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 28.1,\n      \"logprob_residual_pct_MMLU\": 11.2,\n      \"logprob_HellaSwag\": \"feasible in principle (completion scoring) but not implemented\",\n      \"logprob_GSM8K\": \"not feasible — free-form generation has no natural logprob score\",\n      \"model_ranking_stable_across_precision_MMLU\": true,\n      \"model_ranking_stable_across_precision_GSM8K\": false,\n      \"3way_interactions_total_MMLU_pct\": 4.2,\n      \"3way_interactions_total_ARC_pct\": 6.8,\n      \"3way_interactions_total_GSM8K_pct\": 2.1,\n      \"residual_after_3way_MMLU_pct\": 15.3,\n      \"IRT_items_G80_MMLU\": 116,\n      \"Gtheory_items_G80_MMLU\": 510,\n      \"IRT_to_Gtheory_ratio\": 4.4,\n      \"IRT_comparison_feasible_on\": \"MMLU only — other benchmarks lack sufficient data or have pathological structures\",\n      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_MMLU\": 0.99,\n      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_ARC\": 0.94,\n      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_GSM8K\": 0.78,\n      \"fixed_vs_random_delta_MMLU_pp\": 2.8,\n      \"fixed_vs_random_delta_GSM8K_pp\": 8.4,\n      \"bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_MMLU\": 0.31,\n      \"bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_GSM8K\": 0.68,\n      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_MMLU\": 0.98,\n      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_ARC\": 1.03,\n      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_GSM8K\": 1.14,\n      \"MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_item_id_pp\": 2.9,\n      \"MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_model_x_item_pp\": 1.4,\n      \"G_at_14K_items_MMLU_projection\": 0.987,\n      \"G_at_full_ARC_2590_items_projection\": 0.953,\n      \"G_at_full_GSM8K_1319_items_projection\": 0.812,\n      \"logprob_cross_benchmark\": \"only MMLU and ARC feasible, insufficient for cross-benchmark claims\",\n      \"compute_cost_per_G80_MMLU\": \"~$45 (510 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds)\",\n      \"compute_cost_per_G80_GSM8K\": \"~$210 (1150 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds, longer generation)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for All Variance Components (MMLU, 4 models)\",\n        \"description\": \"7 variance components × point estimate + 95% CI. item_id: 42.1% [36.0, 48.8], model×item: 33.6% [28.4, 37.9], residual: 19.5% [16.2, 23.1]. All CIs exclude zero for top-4 components. prompt×item and seed×item CIs overlap.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: REML vs Henderson I Comparison Across Benchmarks\",\n        \"description\": \"4 benchmarks × 7 components × Henderson I estimate + REML estimate + Δ. MMLU: all Δ < 0.5pp. ARC: Δ < 1.2pp. HellaSwag: precision×item oscillates. GSM8K: seed×item diverges (1.8% vs clamped 0%). Conclusion: Henderson I reliable for MC benchmarks; caution needed for free-form generation.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements (MMLU)\",\n        \"description\": \"Reliability threshold × IRT items × G-theory items × ratio. G≥0.70: 55 vs 210 (3.8×). G≥0.80: 116 vs 510 (4.4×). G≥0.90: 280 vs 1350 (4.8×). Decomposition of G-theory excess: prompt×item accounts for 22%, seed×item 23%, residual 55% of additional items.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Fixed vs Random Sensitivity Analysis\",\n        \"description\": \"4 benchmarks × top-4 components × random estimate + fixed estimate + Δ. MMLU Δ < 3pp (robust). GSM8K Δ = 8.4pp for item_id (NOT robust — temperature reclassification redistributes 8pp from temperature to residual).\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison with Bootstrap CIs\",\n        \"description\": \"4 benchmarks × items for G≥0.80 [95% CI]. MMLU: 510 [430, 610]. ARC: 340 [260, 470]. HellaSwag: 280 [210, 380]. GSM8K: 1150 [600, 2100]. The GSM8K CI spans 3.5×, making the point estimate practically meaningless.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-004 is the analytical core of the paper under worst tier. The MMLU-specific analyses are strong: bootstrap CIs confirm top-3 component ordering, IRT comparison produces the clean 4.4× ratio, REML validates Henderson I, and the binary data robustness checks all pass. These form the backbone of the paper.\\nCross-benchmark analyses reveal the \\\"irreducibly benchmark-specific\\\" finding but cannot support strong quantitative claims due to wide CIs and benchmark-specific pathologies. GSM8K is particularly problematic: high residual → wide CIs → unreliable D-study projections → fixed-vs-random sensitivity failure. The paper should present GSM8K results as \\\"illustrative of pathological variance structure in free-form generation\\\" rather than as reliable G-theory estimates.\\nThe IRT vs G-theory comparison is limited to MMLU (the only benchmark with full design + clean variance structure + IRT comparability). This is sufficient for a strong numbered contribution but prevents claiming \\\"cross-benchmark IRT comparison.\\\"\\nKey paper structure implication: MMLU analysis (exp-001 + exp-004 MMLU-specific) occupies 60-70% of the results section. Cross-benchmark analysis (exp-002 + exp-004 cross-benchmark) occupies 30-40% as an \\\"exploratory extension\\\" with explicit caveats about reduced design, limited benchmarks, and pathological structures.\\n\\n\\nMMLU analyses are strong and largely replicate exp-001 findings with bootstrap CIs and additional robustness checks. The IRT vs G-theory comparison (4.4× item ratio) is clean on MMLU and becomes a primary contribution.\\nCross-benchmark analyses are degraded by three compounding factors: (1) Only 4 benchmarks (no MATH) with only 4 models — limited statistical power for cross-benchmark generalizations and wide bootstrap CIs. (2) GSM8K's pathological variance structure (residual >40%, negative REML estimates) makes several analyses unreliable or uninterpretable on that benchmark. The high residual means G-theory components are estimated with low precision, widening CIs and making D-study projections impractical (CI for G80 items: [600, 2100]). (3) Fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis fails on GSM8K (Δ>5pp), meaning the methodological choice of treating temperature as random vs fixed materially affects results — another benchmark-specific pathology.\\nREML diverges from Henderson I on GSM8K because the balanced design assumption is stressed by near-zero or negative components. Henderson I is unbiased for balanced designs but produces interpretable results even when components are near-zero; REML tries to find ML estimates and can produce negative values that must be clamped. This divergence is itself a finding: Henderson I is reliable for well-behaved MC benchmarks but may be problematic for free-form generation benchmarks.\\nThe logprob analysis cannot extend beyond MC formats (MMLU, ARC) because free-form and completion tasks lack a natural single-token probability score. This means the \\\"Evaluation Metrics\\\" claim in the title is only partially supported, and the title may need adjustment per MD9.\\nBootstrap vs jackknife agreement is good on MMLU/ARC (ratio 0.98-1.03) but marginal on GSM8K (ratio 1.14), suggesting bootstrap CIs on GSM8K are slightly anti-conservative due to non-independence in the stochastic generation process.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"14B model extension\",\n    \"description\": \"Blocked. Depends on exp-003 (hardware) which is blocked, and requires ≥48GB VRAM GPUs not currently allocated. No 14B model data is collected.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n      \"n_14B_models_tested\": 0,\n      \"scale_analysis_completed\": false,\n      \"reason\": \"Depends on exp-003 (blocked); requires high-VRAM GPU not available\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. Limitation statement: \\\"Our analysis is limited to 7-9B parameter open-weight models. Whether variance structure changes with model scale is an important open question — larger models may show reduced model×item interaction if they converge toward similar capability profiles, or increased interaction if scale enables more specialized item-level behaviors.\\\"\\n\\n\\nexp-005 has two blocking dependencies: (1) it was designed to run on datacenter GPUs at BF16/FP16 after exp-003 establishes the multi-hardware baseline, and (2) 14B models require ≥48GB VRAM for comfortable inference with vLLM, which is available on the existing RTX PRO 6000 (96GB) but the GPU budget is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1. Even if the hardware dependency were relaxed, the compute hours (100h) compete directly with exp-002's needs. Under worst-tier resource constraints, P1 experiments are systematically deprioritized in favor of P0 experiments.\\nImpact on paper: The \\\"model scale\\\" dimension is entirely absent. The paper must state \\\"all models are 7-9B parameter scale\\\" as a limitation. This is defensible — the paper studies variance structure, not scaling laws — but reviewers may flag it. The planned comparison between dense (Qwen3-14B) and reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B) architectures is lost. This comparison would have been interesting for the model×item interaction analysis (does distillation change the interaction pattern?) but is not essential for the core claims.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Fine-tuning G-study\",\n    \"description\": \"Blocked by compute reallocation. The 1,728 fine-tuning runs (432 per task × 4 tasks) require ~200 GPU hours on westd-32570, which is fully allocated to exp-002 Phase 1 and exp-001 post-processing. Even partial completion (1 task) would consume 50+ hours.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n      \"n_finetuning_runs_completed\": 0,\n      \"tasks_completed\": 0,\n      \"reason\": \"GPU budget fully consumed by P0 experiments (exp-002 Phase 1)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. The fine-tuning extension is entirely absent from the paper. Per existing framing directives, this was already downgraded to Discussion section. Cite Bui et al. (2025) for evidence that fine-tuning variance structure differs substantially (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) and position as motivated future work.\\n\\n\\nexp-006 is independent (no experimental dependencies) but competes for the same GPU resource. Under worst-tier constraints, exp-002 Phase 1 alone consumes 200-250 GPU hours (4 models × 4 benchmarks × ~15h per model-benchmark combination with the full prompt/seed/ordering crossing). With exp-001 post-processing reruns and validation checks, the total GPU budget is exhausted before exp-006 can start.\\nEven if exp-006 were partially completed (e.g., SST-2 only), the results would be insufficient for even a Discussion-section preliminary observation — a single fine-tuning task cannot support variance structure comparison between inference and fine-tuning paradigms.\\nImpact on paper: Fine-tuning analysis is mentioned as future work in Discussion. The paper explicitly qualifies: \\\"Our analysis addresses inference-time evaluation only. Fine-tuning variance structure — where learning rate, data order, and seed interact with task-specific loss landscapes — is a qualitatively different problem that we leave for future work.\\\" Per D006 Dream #3 directive, fine-tuning was already downgraded to Discussion-section preliminary observation, so blockage has minimal impact on paper structure.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LayerCast precision study\",\n    \"description\": \"Deprioritized (P2). Not started due to GPU budget exhaustion from P0 experiments. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as a variance mitigation strategy is not tested.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n      \"layercast_tested\": false,\n      \"variance_reduction_quantified\": false,\n      \"reason\": \"P2 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. Minimal impact on paper — precision is already shown to be negligible under binary scoring. LayerCast is mentioned as a potential mitigation for text-exact-match scoring in the Discussion section.\\n\\n\\nexp-007 is the lowest-priority experiment (P2) and depends on exp-001 being complete (it is). However, the 30 GPU hours required compete with exp-002 Phase 1 needs. Under worst-tier constraints, all discretionary GPU budget is consumed by the P0 pipeline. Additionally, the scientific value of LayerCast testing is reduced once BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis (already completed in exp-001) shows BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) — if standard precision choices don't matter, testing a mixed-precision strategy is low priority.\\nImpact on paper: No variance mitigation strategy is tested. The paper's D-study budgets address \\\"how many items/prompts/seeds\\\" but not \\\"how to reduce precision variance.\\\" This is consistent with the finding that precision contributes <0.1% variance under binary scoring — there is nothing to mitigate. The paper can note: \\\"LayerCast and other mixed-precision strategies may be relevant for text-exact-match scoring where precision×temperature interaction is detectable (1.3%).\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002 Phase 2)\",\n    \"description\": \"Blocked. MATH was merged into exp-002 as the 5th benchmark in Phase 2, which is blocked by answer extraction pipeline issues. Free-form symbolic math answer normalization (handling equivalent forms: \\\"1/2\\\", \\\"0.5\\\", \\\"\\\\\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\", \\\"0.500\\\") requires engineering work that stalls.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"blocked\",\n      \"math_items_collected\": 0,\n      \"answer_extraction_pipeline_status\": \"incomplete — symbolic normalization handles ~70% of MATH answers correctly, remaining 30% require regex/sympy extensions\",\n      \"reason\": \"Merged into exp-002 Phase 2; blocked by answer normalization engineering\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"No analysis possible. The paper's cross-benchmark analysis is limited to 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K) spanning 3 formats (MC, completion, free-form) but with only 1-2 benchmarks per format. Within-format gradient testing is limited to MC (MMLU vs ARC = 2 data points). The \\\"benchmark-specific fingerprints\\\" finding survives but is less compelling without a within-format comparator for free-form tasks.\\n\\nMATH benchmark presents unique data engineering challenges compared to MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) and even compared to GSM8K (which has simpler numerical answers). MATH answers include symbolic expressions, fractions, roots, and multiple equivalent forms that require robust normalization. The extraction pipeline handles simple numerical answers (GSM8K-style) but fails on ~30% of MATH items where the answer is symbolic. Building a robust sympy-based normalization pipeline is estimated at 20-30 engineering hours — work that is deprioritized under worst-tier constraints where the focus is on completing Phase 1 analysis and writing.\\nImpact on paper: The absence of MATH critically undermines the within-format gradient test. With only GSM8K as a free-form benchmark, the within-format gradient analysis has no free-form comparator. The paper cannot distinguish whether GSM8K's low item_id% (35.4%) is due to free-form format, mathematical reasoning task type, or stochastic generation. This ambiguity is a significant limitation for the \\\"benchmark-specific fingerprints\\\" framing — without MATH, the paper cannot determine whether the fingerprint is driven by format or task content within the free-form category.\\nThe paper addresses this by: (a) acknowledging the limitation explicitly, (b) noting that MATH's absence prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and (c) framing MATH as the highest-priority extension for future work. The \\\"benchmark-specific fingerprints\\\" claim is still supported by the MC benchmarks (MMLU vs ARC have different structures despite same format) and the cross-format comparison (MC vs completion vs free-form), but the within-format argument is weaker.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":227,"startLine":1,"totalLines":227}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011VEMgPfid4DJ9h56HoDai3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n5\t- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n6\t- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n7\t\n8\t## Title\n9\tReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 1 — Model-Conditional Item Dominance**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in single-model LLM evaluation (61.1% on MMLU), but model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest source when model is treated as a crossed facet. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific: two models with similar aggregate accuracy (Llama 66.4% vs Gemma 73.2%) disagree dramatically at the item level — item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 38% (cross-model) as model×item absorbs the model-conditional portion. This means single-model variance decompositions overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific item difficulty. Cross-benchmark gradient (pending exp-002) is hypothesized to further modulate this structure.\n14\t**Empirical anchors (exp-001, 4 models, balanced bf16, 345,600 records)**: Single-model item_id=61.1%, cross-model item_id=42.1% [36.0-48.8%] (↓19pp), model×item=33.6% [28.4-37.9%] (second-largest source, increased from 3-model 31.7%). G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861]. D-study prediction validated out-of-sample: 3-model predicted G=0.816 for n=4, actual=0.820 (Δ=0.004). Forward projection: 5 models → G=0.848, 8→0.893, 10→0.910 (G≥0.90 requires ~10 models). Residual dropped 24%→19.5% as 4th model split residual into model×item. Per-model interaction: Mistral 29.25% (lowest accuracy 63.8%) > Qwen 27.11% > Gemma 23.95% > Llama 19.69%.\n15\t**REML robustness**: REML-EM (500 iter, practical convergence Δ<0.003pp) confirms Henderson I: all components differ <0.5pp. Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs. Dataset B differences (item_id 38→52%, model×item 26→12%) due to design differences (factor selection), not estimator bias — measurement design choices themselves affect observed variance structure.\n16\t**Completed robustness**: BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity confirms precision→0.0006% without FP32 (FP32 is sole source of precision variance); leave-one-model-out (matched-spec) shows G_item range 0.747-0.793 across 3-model subsets (spread=0.046) — all below 0.80, confirming 4 models is the minimum for reliable cross-model measurement. LOMO also confirms item_id dominance and model×item as second-largest source are stable across all subsets.\n17\t**GLMM cross-check (dual-analysis, must report both)**: Two complementary analyses:\n18\t(i) *Marginal logit ANOVA*: All 7 grouped component rankings preserved exactly (ρ=1.0), consistent with MQL-1 prediction that logit-scaling is approximately constant at p̄=0.70.\n19\t(ii) *VB GLMM (25-item subsample)*: Ranking reversal on top-2 — model:item_id=47.3% > item_id=22.8% on logit scale (ρ=0.571, p=0.18). Attributable to model underspecification (missing 4 item-interaction terms in VB model) + subsample.\n20\t**Paper must present BOTH honestly** — \"marginal logit ANOVA preserves all rankings; VB GLMM shows differences attributable to model underspecification.\" Do NOT overwrite ρ=0.571 with ρ=1.0. Implication: model×item interaction may be even more important than Henderson I indicates on logit scale — strengthens Claim 1's core message.\n21\t\n22\t**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n23\t\n24\t**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent and temperature-conditional. Binary correct: G=0.611 (200 items), G≥0.80 requires ~510 items (all facets random) or ~414 items (practical: precision+temperature fixed). Text-exact-match: G=0.007 (temperature dominates at 79.7%), unreliable at any temperature >0. Temperature-stratified analysis reveals binary G degrades monotonically (T=0.0: 0.760 → T=0.7: 0.549). G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116) because it accounts for prompt×item (4.6%), seed×item (4.7%), and residual (21.6%) dimensions that IRT ignores. Current practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items) yield G=0.611 — below the 0.80 threshold for dependable measurement.\n25\t\n26\t## Contributions (4 条，明确区分)\n27\t\n28\t1. **Model×item interaction as the hidden dominant source**: In a balanced 4-model design (345,600 records), model×item interaction (33.6%, CI [28.4-37.9%]) is the second-largest variance source, while item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 42%. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific — single-model G-studies overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific difficulty. G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861] with 4 models; D-study prediction validated out-of-sample (3-model predicted 0.816, actual 0.820, Δ=0.004). Reaching G≥0.90 requires ~10 models. [Note: position as quantification of a phenomenon expected from measurement theory, not as a surprising discovery. The novelty is the magnitude, the out-of-sample D-study validation, and the cross-model generalizability implications.]\n29\t\n30\t2. **Precision separability with metric qualification**: Precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies. Under text-exact-match scoring, precision×temperature reaches 4.15%, consistent with floating-point non-associativity. Separability is confirmed as metric-conditional. [Note: scope to \"numerical precision on single GPU architecture\" — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. This is a confirmatory secondary finding, not a primary contribution. Pending BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity to isolate FP32/vLLM confound.]\n31\t\n32\t3. **IRT vs G-theory: the cost of multi-facet reliability**: The first empirical quantification of the multi-facet reliability cost for LLM evaluation: G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116 for MMLU) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. At IRT's recommended 116 items, G-theory G is only 0.529. This quantifies the \"hidden cost\" of evaluation dimensions invisible to IRT. [Note: keep as numbered contribution — Dream + Reviewer consensus this is one of the paper's strongest findings. Wording adjusted from \"first direct comparison\" to \"first empirical quantification\" per SF6 resolution.]\n33\t\n34\t4. **Practitioner toolkit**: Open-source G-theory variance decomposition pipeline + D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Includes: (a) benchmark-specific reproducibility budget tables (items × prompts × seeds for target G), (b) temperature-stratified reliability guide, (c) model-as-facet analysis template. Addresses \"purely diagnostic\" criticism — provides actionable tools, not just warnings.\n35\t\n36\t## Methodological Robustness Package (6 验证分析)\n37\tAll completed on Llama 239,200 records:\n38\t- **Binary data not artifact**: Null simulation item_id=0.08% vs observed 61.1% (737.5×); obs/Bernoulli ratio=1.0008\n39\t- **Fixed vs random insensitive**: Binary G ranges 0.611-0.664 across all facet classification choices\n40\t- **Bootstrap = jackknife**: CI width ratio 0.96-1.04, independence violation negligible\n41\t- **Temperature stratification**: Binary G degrades T=0.0 (0.760) → T=0.7 (0.549); text metric unreliable at all T>0\n42\t- **D-study formula verified**: Monotonicity assertion added; min_items_G80=414 (practical) / 510 (all random)\n43\t- **IRT comparison**: G-theory 3.6-4.4× more conservative than IRT; difference attributable to prompt/seed/ordering dimensions\n44\t- **REML vs Henderson I**: REML-EM (500 iter) confirms all components within <0.5pp of Henderson I; Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs\n45\t- **BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity**: Excluding FP32, precision effect drops from 0.4% to 0.0006% (precision×item: 1.3%→0.04%). FP32 anomaly is entirely a vLLM enforce_eager artifact, not a fundamental precision effect. BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp accuracy)\n46\t- **Leave-one-model-out stability**: All 3-model subsets preserve item_id dominance (39-45%) and model×item as second-largest (32-37%). G_item range 0.747-0.793 (spread=0.046), all below 0.80 — confirming 4 models is the minimum configuration for G≥0.80. This strengthens the D-study \"need ~10 models for G≥0.90\" prediction.\n47\t\n48\t## Story Spine\n49\t\n50\t**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n51\t\n52\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n53\t\n54\t**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n55\t\n56\t**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of 345,600 evaluation records across 4 models on MMLU (200 items × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings), item selection accounts for 42% of variance when model is treated as a crossed facet — but model×item interaction (34%) emerges as the second-largest source, revealing that item difficulty rankings are substantially model-specific (G_item=0.82, requiring ~10 models for G≥0.90). A D-study from 3 models predicted the 4-model G coefficient within 0.004 — out-of-sample validation of the D-study methodology. G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT for reliable measurement because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. Numerical precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%). We release an open-source G-theory pipeline and D-study budget calculator.\"\n57\t\n58\t## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n59\t\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n60\t\n61\t## Narrative Strategy\n62\t- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n63\t- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n64\t- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n65\t- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n66\t- **Figure plan** (5 figures):\n67\t  1. **Stacked-bar: single-model vs cross-model variance decomposition** — item_id shrinks from 61% to 42%, model×item appears at 34%, residual drops 24%→20%. The \"reveal\" figure.\n68\t  2. **G-theory crossed design diagram** — facets, levels, crossing structure\n69\t  3. **D-study projection curves** — G vs item count for binary (practical vs all-random) and text metric. Shows G≥0.80 threshold at ~414/510 items.\n70\t  4. **IRT vs G-theory item requirement comparison** — bar chart showing 116 (IRT) vs 510 (G-theory) items, with breakdown of where the extra items go (prompt×item, seed×item, residual)\n71\t  5. **Temperature-stratified G coefficient heatmap** — binary vs text metric × 3 temperatures, showing metric×temperature interaction\n72\t- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n73\t  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n74\t  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n75\t  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n76\t  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n77\t\n78\t## Venue Fit Risks\n79\t- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n80\t- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n81\t- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n82\t- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n83\t\n84\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n85\t1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n86\t2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n87\t3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n88\t4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n89\t5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n90\t6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n91\t7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n92\t8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n93\t9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n94\t10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n95\t11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n96\t12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n97\t13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n98\t14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n99\t15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n100\t\n101\t## Design Decisions\n102\t- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n103\t- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n104\t- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n105\t- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n106\t- **Model as crossed quasi-random facet**: In exp-001 analysis, model is treated as a crossed quasi-random facet — purposive sample from 4 major architecture families (Llama, Gemma, Mistral, Qwen), not a random draw from all LLMs. Paper must explicitly state selection criteria, discuss purposive vs random sampling implications, and present model-as-fixed sensitivity analysis. D-study Δ=0.004 out-of-sample prediction supports exchangeability assumption. Plan to add 2-3 models (Yi-1.5-9B, OLMo-2-7B, etc.) to strengthen to 6-7 models per MF2 resolution.\n107\t- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n108\t- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n109\t\n110\t## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n111\t\n112\t### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n113\t**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n114\t**Defense strategy (upgraded per Round 1 MF1 + Reviewer + GLMM results)**: Four-line defense, reordered by strength:\n115\t(a) **PRIMARY — Simulation-based recovery** ✅ DONE (matched design verified): Probit latent model → binary, 100 replications × {50,100,200} items × {0.5-0.8} accuracy. **Strongest finding (matched 1,296-condition design): binary ρ=0.732 ≈ continuous ρ=0.741 at n=200 (Δ=0.009) — binary binarization adds NO ranking distortion beyond what continuous data already has.** Henderson I identifies item_id as #1 systematic source in 99-100% of runs. Top-3/top-5 accuracy weaker (55-76% / 58-82%) — do NOT claim \"ranking recovery equivalent\", instead: \"robustly identifies dominant source; smaller component ordering comparable between binary and continuous.\" **Paper must discuss**: (i) residual inflation (latent 16%→~40%, Bernoulli within-cell variance); (ii) \"no-residual\" Spearman is correct metric; (iii) CI coverage slightly anti-conservative at high accuracy (86.4% at p=0.8).\n116\t(b) **COMPLEMENTARY — GLMM logistic-link**: GLMM (Jiang & Skorupski 2017) on 25-item MMLU subsample showed **ranking reversal**: model:item_id #1 (47.3%) > item_id #2 (22.8%) on logit scale, vs Henderson I item_id #1 (42.1%) > model:item_id #2 (33.6%). Spearman ρ=0.571, p=0.18 — rankings NOT statistically consistent. BUT: macro-level agreement holds (item-related >> condition effects in both). Reversal is theoretically expected — logit link amplifies DIF (differential item functioning) for extreme-difficulty items. **Paper framing**: \"Henderson I and GLMM agree on macro structure; differ on item_id vs model:item_id ordering due to scale properties of logit link. This is a complementary perspective, not a contradiction. GLMM suggests model:item interaction may be even more dominant than Henderson I indicates.\" Do NOT claim GLMM \"validates\" Henderson I.\n117\t(c) **Bernoulli-adjusted decomposition**: ✅ DONE. Percentage decomposition inherently normalized by p(1-p) ceiling. Per-model adjustment preserved interaction gradient (Mistral rank drops from #1→#3 but interaction still substantial). Cross-accuracy resampling (0.62-0.72) shows item_id% stable (r=-0.048 with accuracy).\n118\t(d) **Logprob robustness check**: Continuous metric analysis showing gradient preserved — bypasses binary assumption entirely.\n119\t(e) **Methods subsection**: \"Binary Data Assumptions\" discussing Henderson I bias properties for balanced designs, citing Brennan Ch.12, Shavelson et al. 1989, Mushquash & O'Connor 2006, Lalor et al. 2019 (IRT on binary NLP data). Must note GLMM residual drops to 0.004% as Bernoulli variance absorbed by link function.\n120\t\n121\t### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n122\t**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n123\t**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n124\t\n125\t### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n126\t**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n127\t**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n128\t\n129\t### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n130\t**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n131\t**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n132\t\n133\t### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n134\t**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n135\t**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n136\t\n137\t### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n138\t**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n139\t**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n140\t\n141\t### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n142\t**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n143\t**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n144\t\n145\t### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n146\t**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n147\t**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n148\t\n149\t### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n150\t**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n151\t**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n152\t\n153\t## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\n154\tKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n155\t- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n156\t- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n157\t- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n158\t- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n159\t- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n160\t- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n161\t- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n162\t- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical, Ch.12 on binary data)\n163\t- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n164\t- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n165\t- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n166\t- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n167\t- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n168\t- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n169\t- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n170\t- **Sclar et al. (2024, ICLR)** — Up to 76% accuracy swing from prompt formatting; directly relevant to prompt×item variance [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged as missing]\n171\t- **Kunievsky & Evans (2025)** — Alternative decomposition: intent/articulation/model uncertainty; competing framework to G-theory [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged]\n172\t- **Bui et al. (2025)** — Extreme seed sensitivity in fine-tuning (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) [Dream #3: relevant to exp-006]\n173\t- **Jiang & Skorupski (2017)** — Logistic-link generalized G-theory for binary data [Dream #3 worst: methodological defense for REML on binary]\n174\t- **Mushquash & O'Connor (2006)** — Binary-data G-theory [Dream #3: classical psychometrics gap]\n175\t- **Shavelson et al. (1989)** — Binary response G-theory treatment [Dream #3: classical psychometrics]\n176\t\n177\t## Dream #3 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D006)\n178\tPost-analysis framing adjustments based on three-tier consistent feedback:\n179\t1. **Center gradient + model×item as primary contributions** — infrastructure crossing is confirmatory secondary\n180\t2. **Position explicitly as empirical extension of Mesting (2026)** with novel findings (model×item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation, IRT comparison), not a new framework. Remove \"first\" claims where not defensible.\n181\t3. **Scope Claim 2 to \"precision separability\"** — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. Single GPU architecture.\n182\t4. **Item dominance is psychometrically expected** — contribution is quantification of magnitude, metric-conditionality, and D-study implications\n183\t5. **Fine-tuning (exp-006) downgraded** to Discussion-section preliminary observation, not a numbered contribution\n184\t6. **Add explicit judge-facet exclusion discussion** vs Mesting: we fix judge to isolate other factors; Mesting's 43.8% judge variance would dominate\n185\t7. **Practitioner toolkit must be concrete**: specify released scripts, D-study calculator, budget lookup tables\n186\t8. **Figures are non-negotiable**: 5 planned figures address all-tier \"zero figures\" criticism\n187\t\n188\t## Dream #4 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D008)\n189\tPost-analysis adjustments based on Dream #4 (best 7, median 6, worst 5):\n190\t1. **NEW — Format confound in gradient** [三档一致]: MC (MMLU, ARC) vs free-form (GSM8K, MATH) vs completion (HellaSwag) format differences may partially drive gradient. Defense: (a) within-format gradient analysis (MMLU→ARC within MC; GSM8K→MATH within free-form) when exp-002 data available; (b) explicit Discussion paragraph on format as potential confound; (c) if within-format gradient preserved, format is not the driver.\n191\t2. **D-study code documentation**: Released code must include joint optimization (not just single-facet sweeps). Fix before submission.\n192\t3. **Bibliography**: Add CAT (Computerized Adaptive Testing) and MTMM (Multi-Trait Multi-Method) references per median tier.\n193\t4. **Data consistency audit**: Ensure all numbers in paper match analysis outputs exactly — multiple tiers flagged inconsistencies between code/text (residual values, record counts, LOMO spread). Pre-submission checklist item.\n194\t5. **Sclar et al. discrepancy**: Our prompt×item is 3.8-4.6% vs Sclar's 76% accuracy swing. Explain: Sclar measures max-min across adversarial formatting; we measure variance across representative templates. Different questions, compatible answers.\n195\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02938283-148b-4d87-8e7a-0555aca65786","uuid":"31a99eb0-c1ff-4ef9-88aa-69fe72b54e0d","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:24:32.652Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122449/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n\n## Title\nReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 — Model-Conditional Item Dominance**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in single-model LLM evaluation (61.1% on MMLU), but model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest source when model is treated as a crossed facet. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific: two models with similar aggregate accuracy (Llama 66.4% vs Gemma 73.2%) disagree dramatically at the item level — item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 38% (cross-model) as model×item absorbs the model-conditional portion. This means single-model variance decompositions overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific item difficulty. Cross-benchmark gradient (pending exp-002) is hypothesized to further modulate this structure.\n**Empirical anchors (exp-001, 4 models, balanced bf16, 345,600 records)**: Single-model item_id=61.1%, cross-model item_id=42.1% [36.0-48.8%] (↓19pp), model×item=33.6% [28.4-37.9%] (second-largest source, increased from 3-model 31.7%). G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861]. D-study prediction validated out-of-sample: 3-model predicted G=0.816 for n=4, actual=0.820 (Δ=0.004). Forward projection: 5 models → G=0.848, 8→0.893, 10→0.910 (G≥0.90 requires ~10 models). Residual dropped 24%→19.5% as 4th model split residual into model×item. Per-model interaction: Mistral 29.25% (lowest accuracy 63.8%) > Qwen 27.11% > Gemma 23.95% > Llama 19.69%.\n**REML robustness**: REML-EM (500 iter, practical convergence Δ<0.003pp) confirms Henderson I: all components differ <0.5pp. Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs. Dataset B differences (item_id 38→52%, model×item 26→12%) due to design differences (factor selection), not estimator bias — measurement design choices themselves affect observed variance structure.\n**Completed robustness**: BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity confirms precision→0.0006% without FP32 (FP32 is sole source of precision variance); leave-one-model-out (matched-spec) shows G_item range 0.747-0.793 across 3-model subsets (spread=0.046) — all below 0.80, confirming 4 models is the minimum for reliable cross-model measurement. LOMO also confirms item_id dominance and model×item as second-largest source are stable across all subsets.\n**GLMM cross-check (dual-analysis, must report both)**: Two complementary analyses:\n(i) *Marginal logit ANOVA*: All 7 grouped component rankings preserved exactly (ρ=1.0), consistent with MQL-1 prediction that logit-scaling is approximately constant at p̄=0.70.\n(ii) *VB GLMM (25-item subsample)*: Ranking reversal on top-2 — model:item_id=47.3% > item_id=22.8% on logit scale (ρ=0.571, p=0.18). Attributable to model underspecification (missing 4 item-interaction terms in VB model) + subsample.\n**Paper must present BOTH honestly** — \"marginal logit ANOVA preserves all rankings; VB GLMM shows differences attributable to model underspecification.\" Do NOT overwrite ρ=0.571 with ρ=1.0. Implication: model×item interaction may be even more important than Henderson I indicates on logit scale — strengthens Claim 1's core message.\n\n**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n\n**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent and temperature-conditional. Binary correct: G=0.611 (200 items), G≥0.80 requires ~510 items (all facets random) or ~414 items (practical: precision+temperature fixed). Text-exact-match: G=0.007 (temperature dominates at 79.7%), unreliable at any temperature >0. Temperature-stratified analysis reveals binary G degrades monotonically (T=0.0: 0.760 → T=0.7: 0.549). G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116) because it accounts for prompt×item (4.6%), seed×item (4.7%), and residual (21.6%) dimensions that IRT ignores. Current practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items) yield G=0.611 — below the 0.80 threshold for dependable measurement.\n\n## Contributions (4 条，明确区分)\n\n1. **Model×item interaction as the hidden dominant source**: In a balanced 4-model design (345,600 records), model×item interaction (33.6%, CI [28.4-37.9%]) is the second-largest variance source, while item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 42%. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific — single-model G-studies overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific difficulty. G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861] with 4 models; D-study prediction validated out-of-sample (3-model predicted 0.816, actual 0.820, Δ=0.004). Reaching G≥0.90 requires ~10 models. [Note: position as quantification of a phenomenon expected from measurement theory, not as a surprising discovery. The novelty is the magnitude, the out-of-sample D-study validation, and the cross-model generalizability implications.]\n\n2. **Precision separability with metric qualification**: Precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies. Under text-exact-match scoring, precision×temperature reaches 4.15%, consistent with floating-point non-associativity. Separability is confirmed as metric-conditional. [Note: scope to \"numerical precision on single GPU architecture\" — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. This is a confirmatory secondary finding, not a primary contribution. Pending BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity to isolate FP32/vLLM confound.]\n\n3. **IRT vs G-theory: the cost of multi-facet reliability**: The first empirical quantification of the multi-facet reliability cost for LLM evaluation: G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116 for MMLU) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. At IRT's recommended 116 items, G-theory G is only 0.529. This quantifies the \"hidden cost\" of evaluation dimensions invisible to IRT. [Note: keep as numbered contribution — Dream + Reviewer consensus this is one of the paper's strongest findings. Wording adjusted from \"first direct comparison\" to \"first empirical quantification\" per SF6 resolution.]\n\n4. **Practitioner toolkit**: Open-source G-theory variance decomposition pipeline + D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Includes: (a) benchmark-specific reproducibility budget tables (items × prompts × seeds for target G), (b) temperature-stratified reliability guide, (c) model-as-facet analysis template. Addresses \"purely diagnostic\" criticism — provides actionable tools, not just warnings.\n\n## Methodological Robustness Package (6 验证分析)\nAll completed on Llama 239,200 records:\n- **Binary data not artifact**: Null simulation item_id=0.08% vs observed 61.1% (737.5×); obs/Bernoulli ratio=1.0008\n- **Fixed vs random insensitive**: Binary G ranges 0.611-0.664 across all facet classification choices\n- **Bootstrap = jackknife**: CI width ratio 0.96-1.04, independence violation negligible\n- **Temperature stratification**: Binary G degrades T=0.0 (0.760) → T=0.7 (0.549); text metric unreliable at all T>0\n- **D-study formula verified**: Monotonicity assertion added; min_items_G80=414 (practical) / 510 (all random)\n- **IRT comparison**: G-theory 3.6-4.4× more conservative than IRT; difference attributable to prompt/seed/ordering dimensions\n- **REML vs Henderson I**: REML-EM (500 iter) confirms all components within <0.5pp of Henderson I; Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs\n- **BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity**: Excluding FP32, precision effect drops from 0.4% to 0.0006% (precision×item: 1.3%→0.04%). FP32 anomaly is entirely a vLLM enforce_eager artifact, not a fundamental precision effect. BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp accuracy)\n- **Leave-one-model-out stability**: All 3-model subsets preserve item_id dominance (39-45%) and model×item as second-largest (32-37%). G_item range 0.747-0.793 (spread=0.046), all below 0.80 — confirming 4 models is the minimum configuration for G≥0.80. This strengthens the D-study \"need ~10 models for G≥0.90\" prediction.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n\n**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of 345,600 evaluation records across 4 models on MMLU (200 items × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings), item selection accounts for 42% of variance when model is treated as a crossed facet — but model×item interaction (34%) emerges as the second-largest source, revealing that item difficulty rankings are substantially model-specific (G_item=0.82, requiring ~10 models for G≥0.90). A D-study from 3 models predicted the 4-model G coefficient within 0.004 — out-of-sample validation of the D-study methodology. G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT for reliable measurement because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. Numerical precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%). We release an open-source G-theory pipeline and D-study budget calculator.\"\n\n## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n\n## Narrative Strategy\n- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n- **Figure plan** (5 figures):\n  1. **Stacked-bar: single-model vs cross-model variance decomposition** — item_id shrinks from 61% to 42%, model×item appears at 34%, residual drops 24%→20%. The \"reveal\" figure.\n  2. **G-theory crossed design diagram** — facets, levels, crossing structure\n  3. **D-study projection curves** — G vs item count for binary (practical vs all-random) and text metric. Shows G≥0.80 threshold at ~414/510 items.\n  4. **IRT vs G-theory item requirement comparison** — bar chart showing 116 (IRT) vs 510 (G-theory) items, with breakdown of where the extra items go (prompt×item, seed×item, residual)\n  5. **Temperature-stratified G coefficient heatmap** — binary vs text metric × 3 temperatures, showing metric×temperature interaction\n- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n\n## Design Decisions\n- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n- **Model as crossed quasi-random facet**: In exp-001 analysis, model is treated as a crossed quasi-random facet — purposive sample from 4 major architecture families (Llama, Gemma, Mistral, Qwen), not a random draw from all LLMs. Paper must explicitly state selection criteria, discuss purposive vs random sampling implications, and present model-as-fixed sensitivity analysis. D-study Δ=0.004 out-of-sample prediction supports exchangeability assumption. Plan to add 2-3 models (Yi-1.5-9B, OLMo-2-7B, etc.) to strengthen to 6-7 models per MF2 resolution.\n- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n\n## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n\n### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n**Defense strategy (upgraded per Round 1 MF1 + Reviewer + GLMM results)**: Four-line defense, reordered by strength:\n(a) **PRIMARY — Simulation-based recovery** ✅ DONE (matched design verified): Probit latent model → binary, 100 replications × {50,100,200} items × {0.5-0.8} accuracy. **Strongest finding (matched 1,296-condition design): binary ρ=0.732 ≈ continuous ρ=0.741 at n=200 (Δ=0.009) — binary binarization adds NO ranking distortion beyond what continuous data already has.** Henderson I identifies item_id as #1 systematic source in 99-100% of runs. Top-3/top-5 accuracy weaker (55-76% / 58-82%) — do NOT claim \"ranking recovery equivalent\", instead: \"robustly identifies dominant source; smaller component ordering comparable between binary and continuous.\" **Paper must discuss**: (i) residual inflation (latent 16%→~40%, Bernoulli within-cell variance); (ii) \"no-residual\" Spearman is correct metric; (iii) CI coverage slightly anti-conservative at high accuracy (86.4% at p=0.8).\n(b) **COMPLEMENTARY — GLMM logistic-link**: GLMM (Jiang & Skorupski 2017) on 25-item MMLU subsample showed **ranking reversal**: model:item_id #1 (47.3%) > item_id #2 (22.8%) on logit scale, vs Henderson I item_id #1 (42.1%) > model:item_id #2 (33.6%). Spearman ρ=0.571, p=0.18 — rankings NOT statistically consistent. BUT: macro-level agreement holds (item-related >> condition effects in both). Reversal is theoretically expected — logit link amplifies DIF (differential item functioning) for extreme-difficulty items. **Paper framing**: \"Henderson I and GLMM agree on macro structure; differ on item_id vs model:item_id ordering due to scale properties of logit link. This is a complementary perspective, not a contradiction. GLMM suggests model:item interaction may be even more dominant than Henderson I indicates.\" Do NOT claim GLMM \"validates\" Henderson I.\n(c) **Bernoulli-adjusted decomposition**: ✅ DONE. Percentage decomposition inherently normalized by p(1-p) ceiling. Per-model adjustment preserved interaction gradient (Mistral rank drops from #1→#3 but interaction still substantial). Cross-accuracy resampling (0.62-0.72) shows item_id% stable (r=-0.048 with accuracy).\n(d) **Logprob robustness check**: Continuous metric analysis showing gradient preserved — bypasses binary assumption entirely.\n(e) **Methods subsection**: \"Binary Data Assumptions\" discussing Henderson I bias properties for balanced designs, citing Brennan Ch.12, Shavelson et al. 1989, Mushquash & O'Connor 2006, Lalor et al. 2019 (IRT on binary NLP data). Must note GLMM residual drops to 0.004% as Bernoulli variance absorbed by link function.\n\n### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n\n### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n\n### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n\n### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n\n### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n\n### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n\n### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n\n### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n\n## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\nKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical, Ch.12 on binary data)\n- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n- **Sclar et al. (2024, ICLR)** — Up to 76% accuracy swing from prompt formatting; directly relevant to prompt×item variance [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged as missing]\n- **Kunievsky & Evans (2025)** — Alternative decomposition: intent/articulation/model uncertainty; competing framework to G-theory [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged]\n- **Bui et al. (2025)** — Extreme seed sensitivity in fine-tuning (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) [Dream #3: relevant to exp-006]\n- **Jiang & Skorupski (2017)** — Logistic-link generalized G-theory for binary data [Dream #3 worst: methodological defense for REML on binary]\n- **Mushquash & O'Connor (2006)** — Binary-data G-theory [Dream #3: classical psychometrics gap]\n- **Shavelson et al. (1989)** — Binary response G-theory treatment [Dream #3: classical psychometrics]\n\n## Dream #3 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D006)\nPost-analysis framing adjustments based on three-tier consistent feedback:\n1. **Center gradient + model×item as primary contributions** — infrastructure crossing is confirmatory secondary\n2. **Position explicitly as empirical extension of Mesting (2026)** with novel findings (model×item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation, IRT comparison), not a new framework. Remove \"first\" claims where not defensible.\n3. **Scope Claim 2 to \"precision separability\"** — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. Single GPU architecture.\n4. **Item dominance is psychometrically expected** — contribution is quantification of magnitude, metric-conditionality, and D-study implications\n5. **Fine-tuning (exp-006) downgraded** to Discussion-section preliminary observation, not a numbered contribution\n6. **Add explicit judge-facet exclusion discussion** vs Mesting: we fix judge to isolate other factors; Mesting's 43.8% judge variance would dominate\n7. **Practitioner toolkit must be concrete**: specify released scripts, D-study calculator, budget lookup tables\n8. **Figures are non-negotiable**: 5 planned figures address all-tier \"zero figures\" criticism\n\n## Dream #4 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D008)\nPost-analysis adjustments based on Dream #4 (best 7, median 6, worst 5):\n1. **NEW — Format confound in gradient** [三档一致]: MC (MMLU, ARC) vs free-form (GSM8K, MATH) vs completion (HellaSwag) format differences may partially drive gradient. Defense: (a) within-format gradient analysis (MMLU→ARC within MC; GSM8K→MATH within free-form) when exp-002 data available; (b) explicit Discussion paragraph on format as potential confound; (c) if within-format gradient preserved, format is not the driver.\n2. **D-study code documentation**: Released code must include joint optimization (not just single-facet sweeps). Fix before submission.\n3. **Bibliography**: Add CAT (Computerized Adaptive Testing) and MTMM (Multi-Trait Multi-Method) references per median tier.\n4. **Data consistency audit**: Ensure all numbers in paper match analysis outputs exactly — multiple tiers flagged inconsistencies between code/text (residual values, record counts, LOMO spread). Pre-submission checklist item.\n5. **Sclar et al. discrepancy**: Our prompt×item is 3.8-4.6% vs Sclar's 76% accuracy swing. Explain: Sclar measures max-min across adversarial formatting; we measure variance across representative templates. Different questions, compatible answers.\n","numLines":195,"startLine":1,"totalLines":195}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QusQHXuLDc8py8PxbtKbpC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the experiment data and paper framing. Let me also read the distilled research and writing skills to guide paper writing style.\n\nActually, per the instructions, I should just write the paper directly. Let me synthesize the key information:\n\n**Experiments:**\n1. **exp-002**: Multi-benchmark replication - 4 models × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K), Phase 1 only. Key finding: no systematic gradient, benchmark-specific variance fingerprints.\n2. **exp-003**: Multi-hardware G-study - BLOCKED. Single GPU only.\n\n\n3. **exp-004**: Comprehensive analysis - Strong MMLU analyses, cross-benchmark analyses limited. IRT vs G-theory 4.4× ratio. Bootstrap CIs.\n4. **exp-005** through **exp-008**: All blocked - 14B model extension, fine-tuning G-study, LayerCast precision study, and MATH benchmark.\n\nThe narrative guidance is pushing me away from cross-benchmark gradients as the main story and toward focusing on deep MMLU analysis instead, with benchmark-specific findings as secondary support.\n\nNow I'm looking at the variance fingerprints across benchmarks. The hook is that there's no universal reproducibility recipe because variance structure itself differs fundamentally by benchmark. My core contributions are quantifying model×item interactions with D-study validation on MMLU, comparing IRT and G-theory approaches, and showing that variance composition is irreducibly benchmark-specific. Looking at the data, MMLU shows 41.8% item variance and 33.8% model×item interaction, while GSM8K flips this with 35.4% item but 38.9% model×item — that's the fingerprint difference I need to emphasize.\n\nThe generalizability coefficients tell the story too: MMLU achieves 0.821 with just 510 items needed for G≥0.80, but GSM8K requires 1150 items to reach only 0.518. The prompt×item interaction is negligible across all benchmarks (2.4-6.8%), so that's not driving the differences. The gradient permutation test shows no monotonic relationship in item difficulty spread, which rules out a simple difficulty-based explanation for why variance structures diverge. Looking at the generalizability coefficients across the full item pools, MMLU projects to nearly perfect reliability at 0.987, while ARC and GSM8K stabilize around 0.953 and 0.812 respectively. The cost-efficiency analysis shows MMLU requires roughly $45 per reliable measurement compared to $210 for GSM8K, and the D-study predictions are validating well—my three-model forecast of G=0.816 matched the actual G=0.820 almost exactly, suggesting the model is capturing the measurement structure accurately.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the paper structure to tell this story coherently: starting with the abstract and introduction, moving through related work and methods, then diving into the core experiments with MMLU as the centerpiece, examining the model-by-item interactions and cross-benchmark variance patterns, comparing IRT and G-theory approaches, and wrapping up with robustness checks. I need to ground everything in the actual numbers from the data and follow the narrative arc toward the fingerprinting insight rather than getting sidetracked by gradient analysis. I'm noticing the slight variance between exp-002 and exp-001 on MMLU—the reduced design in exp-002 gives item_id at 41.8% versus 42.1%, which is close enough to treat as consistent. Now I'm working through potential titles that capture the core insight about benchmark-specific variance structures, something like \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation\" or emphasizing how variance fingerprints differ across benchmarks rather than following universal patterns.\n\nI'm planning out the full paper structure in LaTeX—abstract, introduction with contributions, related work, then methodology covering G-Theory background and experimental design, followed by results sections diving into MMLU variance decomposition and cross-benchmark comparisons. I'll use a clean article format that's reviewable rather than worrying about specific conference style files.\n\nNow I'm compiling the key numerical results from my experiments, particularly the MMLU deep analysis showing how variance breaks down across items, models, and their interactions, along with the generalizability coefficients and D-study predictions. ≥0.80 requires 510 items [430, 610] (all random) or 414 items (practical)\n- D-study: G≥0.90 requires ~10 models\n- Logprob: item_id 55.3%, model×item 28.1%, residual 11.2%\n- REML vs Henderson I: all <0.5pp\n- Fixed vs random: Δ<2.8pp\n- Bootstrap vs jackknife CI ratio: 0.98\n- 3-way interactions: 4.2%\n- Residual after 3-way: 15.3%\n- Binary data null: item_id=0.08% vs observed 61.1% (737.5×)\n- BF16≈FP16: Δ=0.23pp\n\n**Cross-Benchmark (exp-002):**\n\nNow looking at the variance decomposition across four benchmarks with over 460,000 records total. Item difficulty dominates MMLU and HellaSwag (42-44%), while GSM8K shows much higher residual variance at 43%, suggesting model performance varies more unpredictably on math problems. Generalizability coefficients range from 0.52 on GSM8K to 0.82 on MMLU, with sample size requirements for G≥0.80 varying from 280 items on HellaSwag to 510 on MMLU.\n\nThe gradient analysis reveals non-monotonic patterns across benchmarks—MMLU and ARC differ by 5 percentage points despite similar formats—and permutation testing shows this isn't statistically significant. Comparing IRT and G-theory approaches on MMLU shows G-theory requires 3.8 to 4.8 times fewer items depending on the reliability threshold, with the excess variance split between prompt-item interactions and seed effects. Bootstrap confidence intervals confirm the stability of these estimates, and additional variance components like three-way interactions and precision effects (logprob vs binary) account